{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And Timna was a concubine</b> — Since Scripture did not bother to identify the name of the mother for any of the others, our Rabbis (Genesis Rabbah 82:14) expounded: this is to proclaim the greatness of Abraham our father — how eager people were to attach themselves to his seed. For this Timna was the daughter of chieftains, as it is written, \"And Lotan's sister was Timna.\" She said: I am not worthy to marry you; if only I could be a concubine — as Rashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 36:12). And it is possible that the five sons of Eliphaz were well known to him because he begot them from his wives, while Amalek, because he was the son of a concubine, had no standing among his brothers. He was included among the descendants of Esau by virtue of being from his seed, and Scripture needed to state that his mother was a certain woman who is known to have borne him to Eliphaz, but he is not counted among the sons of Esau and does not dwell with them in Mount Seir. For through the sons of the principal wives he is called \"seed,\" but not through the son of the concubine, since the son of a handmaid does not inherit together with his sons, as his father did. Now, we were commanded regarding the descendants of Esau neither to abhor them (Deuteronomy 23:8) nor to take their land (ibid. 2:5) — and they are all his known sons who dwell in Seir, for they are the ones called Edom after his name. But the son of the concubine is not included among the sons of Esau and is not with them in their land, and we were commanded the opposite concerning him: to abhor him and to blot out his name.\n\nAnd Rashi further wrote: in Chronicles (1 Chronicles 1:36) it counts Timna among the sons of Eliphaz, which teaches that he [Eliphaz] lay with the wife of Seir the Horite, and Timna was born from them, and when she grew up she became his concubine. And this is the meaning of \"And Lotan's sister was Timna\" (Genesis 36:22) — and she is not counted among the sons of Seir because she was his sister from the mother and not from the father. My mind does not accept this, for it would have been proper for Chronicles to say \"and Timna his daughter,\" and why would it count a woman among the sons? Perhaps Scripture is not particular about such a thing when it is well known, for we find there in Chronicles (1 Chronicles 5:29): \"And the sons of Amram: Aaron and Moses and Miriam, and the sons of Aaron: Nadab and Abihu,\" etc. And if so, it is proper to say that this Timna is the daughter of Eliphaz, whom the wife of Seir the Horite bore him after the death of her husband, and she was Lotan's sister from the mother, and Eliphaz took her as a concubine — for a gentile is permitted in his daughter. Or we may say according to the opinion of our Rabbis that the Timna mentioned in Chronicles is Timna the chieftain mentioned below (Genesis 36:40), who is counted there among the sons of Eliphaz, just as Korah is counted, and he is the son of Oholibamah — for both were mamzerim, born from a forbidden union and counted among the sons of the other wife, for it is far-fetched that Scripture would count a woman among the sons in their midst.\n\nAccording to the plain sense, one may suggest that Timna, the concubine of Eliphaz, after she gave birth to Amalek, bore a son and had difficulty in childbirth and died, and she named him Timna in order to commemorate her name, and his father Eliphaz named him Korah. And Scripture did not mention this son of Timna his mother so as not to prolong, for the intent was to count Amalek separately; but the sons of Eliphaz were seven. And Scripture counted the chieftains according to their rank; therefore it placed Kenaz and Korah before Gatam.\n\nAnd I further hold concerning this verse what our Rabbies said among the thirty-two rules (Rule 11) by which the Aggadah is expounded: they said — it was arranged and should have been continuous, but it was divided, as it is written (2 Chronicles 30:18), \"for the multitude of the people,\" etc. And even pursuers of the plain sense say the same in other verses. So too here one may say: \"the sons of Eliphaz: Teman, Omar, Zepho, and Gatam, and Kenaz, and Timna,\" and then it went back and said, \"she was a concubine of Eliphaz, son of Esau, and she bore Amalek to Eliphaz,\" and did not mention the name of the concubine. And the truth is that she is Lotan's sister Timna, and that is the reason Scripture did not mention her name — because it did not wish to say \"and Timna\" twice, for the male and for the female. And so the sons of Eliphaz are seven, and they are the chieftains mentioned for him, but they changed the name of this younger one, because his name was the same as the name of the concubine, so that he would not be thought to be her son, and they called him Korah when he attained the rank of chieftain.\n\nAnd Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Genesis 36:2) that Korah son of Oholibamah the wife of Esau is counted twice, for he is the youngest of the sons of Oholibamah, and Adah raised him — and similarly the sons of Michal daughter of Saul, according to the words of our Rabbies (Sanhedrin 19a). According to this view, the interpretation of the verse in Chronicles would be that Timna bore Amalek, its reading being: and to Timna, Amalek — and the lamed is elided, in the manner of \"and two captains of raiding bands were son of Saul\" (2 Samuel 4:2), meaning: of Saul. But the correct interpretation, in my view, is what I have suggested: and the reason for \"these are the sons of Adah\" follows the majority, for Amalek is not her son — and similarly, \"these are the sons of Jacob who were born to him in Paddan-aram\" (Genesis 35:26), does not refer to Benjamin."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 36, :notes {0 "In the reading of the verse paraphrased mid-comment, Ramban writes 'אומר צפו' (Omar, Zepho) following a common textual variant; the standard Masoretic text reads 'אומר צפו וגעתם וקנז' — the paraphrase preserves his exact sequence."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:40:43.795632282Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}